To extract all 17 apps listed in your output at once, you can run a single automated bash script. This script parses your search output, creates a dedicated folder, and copies every base.apk into it while automatically renaming each file to its clean package name.
## Step 1: Create a target directory
Create a folder in your user space to store all the extracted APK files:

mkdir -p ~/Downloads/waydroid_extracted_apks

## Step 2: Run the extraction loop
Execute this loop directly from your current terminal directory (~/.local/share/waydroid/data/app$). It uses aapt to instantly fetch the clean package name and saves the file properly:

find . -name "base.apk" | while read -r apk_path; do
    # Get the package name using aapt
    pkg_name=$(aapt dump badging "$apk_path" 2>/dev/null | grep "package: name=" | head -n 1 | awk -F"'" '{print $2}')
    
    # Fallback to directory parsing if aapt fails on system apps
    if [ -z "$pkg_name" ]; then
        pkg_name=$(echo "$apk_path" | cut -d/ -f3 | cut -d- -f1)
    fi
    
    echo "Extracting: $pkg_name..."
    cp "$apk_path" ~/Downloads/waydroid_extracted_apks/"$pkg_name".apkdone

## Step 3: Verify the extracted files
Switch over to your target directory and verify that all your apps (Trust Wallet, WhatsApp, MTN Mobile Money, 1xBet, UBA, etc.) have been neatly compiled as flat APKs:

cd ~/Downloads/waydroid_extracted_apks && ls -lh

